          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Harvest primarily signifies the eschatological end of the age, when humanity is gathered and separated: the righteous are received into God's kingdom and the wicked are judged. Evangelism, moral recompense, and blessing are recognized contextual alternatives.\",\"book_summary\":\"Harvest is the end-of-age reaping of humanity, with wheat gathered and chaff burned.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The end-of-age ingathering and judgment of humanity, including the gathering of the righteous and punishment of the wicked.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The end-of-age reaping and separation of humanity, with the righteous gathered and the wicked burned.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"None material. The book states only the primary eschatological sense and does not discuss the consensus's secondary contextual uses, but it does not expressly exclude them.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None.\",\"rationale\":\"The book reproduces the consensus's central identification almost exactly. Its wheat/chaff formulation is a concrete expression of the same twofold end-time gathering and judgment, not a narrowing or replacement of the referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Joel 3:13 emphasizes punitive judgment rather than righteous ingathering, and Matthew 13:39 alone identifies harvest with the end of the age without itself stating the wheat/chaff outcomes. Even so, the printed citations strongly support the entry's core identification, with Matthew 13:39 providing an explicit definition.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
